  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is the Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio?</h2>
    <p>The shoulder-to-hip ratio (SHR) compares the circumference of your shoulders with the circumference of your hips. It is one of the clearest differences between male and female body shape: men&rsquo;s shoulders are usually well wider than their hips, while women&rsquo;s are about the same size.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/shoulder-to-hip-ratio-chart-3d.webp" width="1780" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Shoulder-to-hip ratio chart: 3D women at 0.93, 1.03 and 1.15 and men at 1.06, 1.18 and 1.32"><figcaption>Same hips, different shoulders: ratios for women and men.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="average" aria-labelledby="average-h">
    <h2 id="average-h">Average Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio for Men and Women</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Shoulder-to-hip ratio percentiles</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">5th</th><th scope="col">25th</th><th scope="col">50th</th><th scope="col">75th</th><th scope="col">95th</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>0.92</td><td>0.99</td><td>1.03</td><td>1.07</td><td>1.14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>1.06</td><td>1.13</td><td>1.18</td><td>1.23</td><td>1.30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>The figures come from Hughes and Gallup (2003), who measured 116 young adults with a tape over the shoulders and around the hips. Because the sample was college students, treat them as a guide rather than national norms. For context, larger U.S. Army data put average shoulder circumference at about 46&nbsp;in for men and 40.5&nbsp;in for women.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="shape" aria-labelledby="shape-h">
    <h2 id="shape-h">Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio and Body Shape</h2>
    <p>For women, SHR is one of the simplest ways to separate the main body shapes:</p>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>Above about 1.05:</strong> shoulders wider than hips, typical of the <a href="/inverted-triangle-body-shape/">inverted triangle</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>About 0.96 to 1.05:</strong> balanced, typical of the <a href="/hourglass-body-shape/">hourglass</a> (defined waist) and rectangle (straight waist).</li>
      <li><strong>Below about 0.96:</strong> hips wider than shoulders, typical of the <a href="/pear-body-shape/">pear</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href="/">body shape calculator</a> uses bust, waist and hips to give a full result. For men, a high SHR and a narrow waist make the trapezoid and inverted triangle builds; check yours with the <a href="/male-body-type-calculator/">male body type calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="attractiveness" aria-labelledby="attractiveness-h">
    <h2 id="attractiveness-h">Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio and Attractiveness</h2>
    <p>Studies in evolutionary psychology have linked a higher SHR in men with being rated as more attractive and stronger, much as a lower <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-hip ratio</a> is linked with attractiveness ratings for women. These are averages across raters, not rules, and they say nothing about health.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-measure" aria-labelledby="how-to-measure-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-measure-h">How to Measure Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Measure your shoulders.</strong> Stand straight with your arms relaxed at your sides. Wrap a tape around the widest part of the shoulders, over the deltoids and shoulder blades, keeping it level.</li><li><strong>Measure your hips.</strong> Stand with feet together and wrap the tape around the widest part of the hips and buttocks, level with the floor.</li><li><strong>Divide.</strong> Divide the shoulder measurement by the hip measurement, using the same unit for both.</li></ol>
    <p>You can also compare widths instead of circumferences: shoulder width across the back (see <a href="/average-shoulder-width/">average shoulder width</a>) against hip width at the widest point. Width ratios are larger numbers but tell the same story.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="change" aria-labelledby="change-h">
    <h2 id="change-h">Can You Change It?</h2>
    <p>Shoulder and pelvis bones stop growing in early adulthood, so the frame underneath is fixed. What you can change is the soft tissue: building the deltoids and upper back raises shoulder circumference, and losing fat from the hips and buttocks lowers hip circumference. Try different combinations in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a>, or compare your shoulders with your waist in the <a href="/shoulder-to-waist-ratio-calculator/">shoulder-to-waist ratio calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Shoulder-to-Hip Ratio FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average shoulder-to-hip ratio?</h3></summary><p>In a study of young U.S. adults by Hughes and Gallup, the average shoulder-to-hip ratio (shoulder circumference divided by hip circumference) was 1.18 for men and 1.03 for women. Most men fell between about 1.10 and 1.27 and most women between 0.95 and 1.11.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do you calculate shoulder-to-hip ratio?</h3></summary><p>Measure around the widest part of your shoulders and around the widest part of your hips, in the same unit, then divide the shoulder number by the hip number. For example, 44 ÷ 40 = 1.10.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is a good shoulder-to-hip ratio for a man?</h3></summary><p>There is no medical target, but ratios above about 1.25 read as broad-shouldered and athletic, and research suggests a higher ratio is generally seen as more attractive in men. The average is about 1.18.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is a good shoulder-to-hip ratio for a woman?</h3></summary><p>A ratio close to 1.0, with shoulders and hips about the same size, is typical for women and is part of the balanced look of hourglass and rectangle shapes. There is no healthy or unhealthy value; the ratio describes shape, not health.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is shoulder-to-hip ratio related to body shape?</h3></summary><p>Yes. For women, a ratio well above 1.05 usually means shoulders wider than the hips (inverted triangle), and a ratio below about 0.96 usually means hips wider than the shoulders (pear). For men, a high ratio is part of the trapezoid and inverted triangle builds.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Can you change your shoulder-to-hip ratio?</h3></summary><p>Partly. Building the shoulders and upper back raises the ratio, and losing fat from the hips raises it slightly too. The bone widths of the shoulders and pelvis do not change in adults.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is shoulder-to-hip ratio the same as waist-to-hip ratio?</h3></summary><p>No. Waist-to-hip ratio compares the waist with the hips and is used as a health marker for belly fat. Shoulder-to-hip ratio compares the shoulders with the hips and describes frame and shape.</p></details>
  </section>

